; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Figurate Numbers
; CATEGORY: Number Theory
; LEVEL:    Beginner
; TAGS:     triangular numbers, square numbers, pentagonal numbers, dots, patterns
; SUMMARY:  Draws triangular, square and pentagonal numbers as growing shapes made of dots, with their counts underneath.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Some numbers make shapes. 1, 3, 6, 10, 15 dots pile up into
;   triangles. 1, 4, 9, 16, 25 dots make squares. 1, 5, 12, 22, 35
;   make pentagons, each one wrapped around the last. The Greeks called
;   these "figurate" numbers. Each row of the picture shows one family
;   growing step by step.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Change :SIZES to draw more or fewer shapes in each row,
;   and :GAP to space the dots out.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * nested FOR loops that place dots in rows and columns
;   * DOT and SETPENSIZE to make round counters
;   * walking a pentagon with REPEAT 5 and turning 72 degrees
;   * formulas that predict the count without drawing
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Add a row for hexagonal numbers: 1, 6, 15, 28 (turn 60, walk 6 sides).
;   * Put two triangles of the same size together. What shape do they make?
;     That is why the triangular formula has n * (n + 1) / 2 in it.
; ============================================================

; Triangle number n: row r has r dots, centered on cx.
to triangle.dots :n :cx :top
  for [r 1 :n] [
    for [j 1 :r] [
      dot (list :cx + (:j - (:r + 1) / 2) * :gap  :top - (:r - 1) * :gap * 0.87)
    ]
  ]
end

; Square number n: an n by n block, centered on cx.
to square.dots :n :cx :top
  for [r 1 :n] [
    for [c 1 :n] [
      dot (list :cx + (:c - (:n + 1) / 2) * :gap  :top - (:r - 1) * :gap)
    ]
  ]
end

; Pentagonal number n: pentagons of side 1, 2, ... n-1 gaps, all sharing
; their bottom-left corner. Dots that land on the same spot twice just
; overlap, so we see each one once.
to pentagon.dots :n :cx :bottom
  penup setxy :cx - :gap * (:n - 1) / 2 :bottom
  localmake "corner pos
  dot :corner
  if :n < 2 [stop]      ; careful: FOR [k 2 1] would count DOWN from 2 to 1
  for [k 2 :n] [
    setpos :corner setheading 90
    repeat 5 [
      repeat :k - 1 [forward :gap dot pos]
      left 72
    ]
  ]
end

to caption :cx :y :text
  setlabelheight 15
  penup setxy :cx - 4.5 * count :text :y
  setpencolor [50 50 50]
  label :text
end

to row.title :y :text
  setlabelheight 18
  penup setxy -390 :y setpencolor [50 50 50]
  label :text
end

to show.family :kind :y :color
  localmake "i 0
  foreach :sizes [
    localmake "cx -190 + :i * 120
    setpencolor :color setpensize 8
    if :kind = "triangle [triangle.dots ? :cx :y + 60  caption :cx :y - 15 ? * (? + 1) / 2]
    if :kind = "square [square.dots ? :cx :y + 60  caption :cx :y - 15 ? * ?]
    if :kind = "pentagon [pentagon.dots ? :cx :y + 5  caption :cx :y - 28 ? * (3 * ? - 1) / 2]
    make "i :i + 1
  ]
end

make "sizes [1 2 3 4 5]
make "gap 13

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [252 250 245]
row.title 175 [Triangular]
show.family "triangle 140 [220 90 60]
row.title 15 [Square]
show.family "square -30 [60 120 210]
row.title -175 [Pentagonal]
show.family "pentagon -230 [60 160 90]
; Words between vertical bars keep their spaces and parentheses as typed.
print "|Triangular number n = n * (n + 1) / 2|
print "|Square number n     = n * n|
print "|Pentagonal number n = n * (3n - 1) / 2|
